# CR-031 (audit A5-F3 / PG-F1). Nothing declared end-of-line normalisation, so a checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true — the Git for Windows default — would rewrite every tracked text file with CRLF.
# That is not merely a "scripts fail to start" problem, though shebangs do become `bash\r`:
#
#   - the fenced-payload byte comparison behind the §4 seed identity check would report drift on
#     every seed, failing the exception the whole build rests on;
#   - validate-crew's line-exact matching would stop matching correct rules;
#   - bash-blocker's whole-string case patterns would see a trailing \r that was not there when the
#     patterns were written.
#
# One line prevents all of it, and it is a no-op on Linux. Landed before any Windows work rather
# than as part of it, because the alternative is discovering it as a mystifying red gate after the
# first CRLF checkout has already happened.
#
# Two adjacent Windows traps are absent and should stay that way: zero symlinks in tracked files,
# and all twenty executable shell files declaring the same interpreter line.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the file types where a stray CR is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
